"It's really a short-term measure. What they've agreed is to release 100m barrels of strategic reserves.
"That will be over a four-week period and there'll be a particular front loading of diesel over the next 20 days. So, it is significant," Prof Ó Gallachóir said.
He added: "Just to put that quantity into context, at the moment, the blockade at the Strait of Hormuz is blocking between 15-20m barrels every day in terms of really impacting and restricting the supply of oil.
"And that's what's causing these significant oil prices.
"So, to dampen down the prices in a longer-term way, I mean, essentially the US and Israel need to stop the attacks on Iran and enable the opening up of the Strait of Hormuz."
