## First read

The page told me that the G7 agreed to release 100 million barrels over four months, with early diesel, while leaving unclear how much is additional to March.

At the end I still wanted to know how much diesel was promised, whether any October barrels had moved, and how much of the 100 million was new. I first lost the thread at: “The October 2 statement calls for the immediate and full restoration of navigational rights and principles in the Strait of Hormuz.” [Story view](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fuel/checks/audits/fuel-reserves--2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels-2026-10-03-r7-editor-page.txt)

## The story

The supported story is the G7’s “coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months,” “taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled,” with a “frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days by G7 members and partners” and a pledge to “refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries.” [Joint statement](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fuel/data/sources/fuel-reserves/govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02.txt)

The page has that angle and opening act. Its March paragraph gives the reserve sequence in date order; the next paragraph gives the September 30 to October 2 export sequence. The unresolved amounts are stated plainly at the end. My targeted October 3 searches found no later allocation, delivery notice, or decision by the named actors that moves the event beyond the page. The relevant [IEA listing](https://www.iea.org/news/) still dates its G7 account October 2; the [Energy Department request](https://www.energy.gov/articles/united-states-energy-department-continues-execution-strategic-reserve-release-commitments) sets the next bid deadline at October 6.

For the marked paragraphs:

1. **What happened: yes.** The first paragraph gives the act, schedule, diesel priority, refinery measures, and export pledge. The French ceiling follows immediately.
2. **Why it matters: yes.** The paragraph gives dated pump prices and the people Bessent named.
3. **Easiest wrong reading: yes.** It sets the president’s diesel claim against an agreement with no diesel number, named supplier, or recorded delivery.
4. **Mechanism: yes.** “A barrel counted as crude is not the diesel a pump sells” is a sentence a reader can carry away.
5. **What changed: yes in substance.** The marked paragraph compares March’s pledge and released barrels with October’s timetable; the adjacent paragraph gives the export decision and the president’s refusal of a ban.
6. **Unresolved: yes.** The closing paragraph collects the additional amount, product split, country shares, and delivery as unknowns. [Story view](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fuel/checks/audits/fuel-reserves--2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels-2026-10-03-r7-editor-page.txt)

## The top

The first sentence says what happened, although its three quoted clauses demand attention. The headline does not call all 100 million barrels diesel, and the dek expressly says diesel has no number. No person appears before introduction. The quoted “frontloaded” arrives with its 20-day meaning. I would run this top. [Story view](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fuel/checks/audits/fuel-reserves--2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels-2026-10-03-r7-editor-page.txt)

## Line edits

1. **Minor · new · CUT.** Page bytes: “20 days window for the frontloaded diesel release”. Reason: the dek and opening paragraph already give the window; removing this tile costs the reader no answer. [Story view](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fuel/checks/audits/fuel-reserves--2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels-2026-10-03-r7-editor-page.txt)

## Holes

